<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  M5 SPLASH CORRECTION — the deliberately EMPTY Android system splash icon.

  Why an empty icon instead of the approved emblem:

  Android 12+ always draws a platform-controlled launch surface and cannot be
  told to draw none.  While that surface carried the emblem alone on the brand
  blue, it was a small logo centred in a blue field — which is EXACTLY the
  composition of the earlier, rejected "old splash", and on a real cold launch
  it dominates the screen (measured on the authorized Infinix: 0.79 s-1.9 s of
  the launch) while the approved composition only appears afterwards.  The owner
  therefore perceives old splash -> new splash, which is the one outcome M5 must
  not produce.

  Android also cannot render the approved composition natively: the splash
  background attribute is a plain colour and the icon is scaled into a masked
  slot, so the title and tagline can never reach it.

  So the unavoidable platform surface is reduced to what it can honestly be: the
  approved intro splash's OWN deep-blue field, with no logo, no tile, no text and
  no second design.  The field colour is the first stop of the approved
  artwork's own backdrop (`nt_native_blue`, sampled from the artwork's top edge),
  so the platform surface and the first Flutter frame are byte-identical at the
  top and hand over with no visible seam.

  The ONLY branded splash a user ever sees is then the owner-approved
  composition, which owns the first Flutter frame.

  This drawable must stay transparent.  Putting any artwork back here restores
  the "old splash" perception and fails the M5 splash contract
  (test/features/startup/presentation/m5_clean_splash_test.dart, tests N5/N7).
-->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">
    <solid android:color="@android:color/transparent" />
</shape>
